| header item | actual node indices | summed support |
| --- | --- | ---: |
| bread | [1] | 3 |
| milk | [2, 3] | 3 |

Milk step: actual pattern [1]; conditional path [0] with weight 2; child tree 1; pair emitted as [1, 0] with support 2.
\image(figures/fp_growth_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The shared bread→milk path counts two baskets, while a separate root milk node counts one. Milk’s two header nodes sum to support three; only the shared node contributes the conditional bread path of weight two.)
\alttext(A native rooted tree has root n0, bread n1 with count three, milk n2 below bread with count two and a separate root milk n3 with count one.)

> Why target-node counts give conditional supports

For a target item x, the header lists every tree node labelled x. Those nodes
partition its weighted occurrences by their shared prefix, so summing their counts
gives support(x). For each such node, walk toward the root and keep its ancestors,
excluding x and the root. Weight that entire path by the target node's count.
An ancestor can have a larger count because other baskets stop early or branch
elsewhere; using its count would overstate co-occurrence with x.

The tiny milk node under bread has count two even though bread has count three.
Its conditional base therefore contains bread with weight two. The separate root
milk node has no ancestors and contributes no nonempty conditional path. Mining
the base emits the pair with support two. Native paths are stored nearest ancestor
first; each child builder orders them again by its own weighted frequencies.

More generally, for a current suffix P and a nonempty extension set X drawn from its
retained ancestors, the conditional occurrence accounting gives

$$
\operatorname{support}_{D}(P\cup X)
=\sum_{\substack{\text{conditional paths }B\\X\subseteq B}}\operatorname{weight}(B).
$$
\alttext(The support of a suffix extended by an ancestor itemset equals the sum of target-node weights of conditional paths containing that extension.)

Filtering any conditional item whose weighted support is below the threshold
cannot remove a frequent extension: every superset has no greater support.
A fixed item order within each tree assigns patterns to suffix branches, avoiding duplicates;
a result is finally sorted into ascending IDs and then cardinality/lexicographic
order. The actual emission sequence can differ from this final presentation:
the tiny trace emits {0}, then {1}, then {1,0}, while the returned pair is {0,1}.

Han, Pei and Yin's
[Mining Frequent Patterns without Candidate Generation](https://sigmodrecord.org/2000/06/07/mining-frequent-patterns-without-candidate-generation/),
SIGMOD2000,1–12,
[doi:10.1145/342009.335372](https://doi.org/10.1145/342009.335372), develops the
FP-tree, target-weighted conditional bases and pattern-growth argument.
This implementation uses owned vectors of header node IDs and explicit DFS frames.
It rebuilds conditional trees directly and omits the paper's single-path shortcut.
Avoiding Apriori's candidate join does not mean that counting, filtering or
combinatorial output disappears.

> Preserve the original eight grocery baskets

The native fixture retains the original five item names and all eight baskets.
At minimum count two it returns the same eleven supports as before. Here we
execute the comparison with both Apriori and Eclat, rather than inferring agreement.
All five singleton items survive this threshold; twenty-one retained incidences
share ten nonroot initial-tree nodes. Root count zero is an internal placeholder,
so the drawing labels the root without presenting it as a basket count.

The old schematic put milk at a root of count five. The actual tie rule puts
bread0 before milk1: bread is a root child of count five, its milk child counts
four, and another root milk node counts one. The diagram below comes directly
from those native records.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fp_growth_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/fp_growth.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/eclat.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto rows=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_example_transactions();const auto names=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_example_labels();const auto t=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace(rows,2);const auto a=dm::algorithms::apriori_frequent_itemsets(rows,2),e=dm::algorithms::eclat_frequent_itemsets(rows,2);
  const auto equal=[](const auto&x,const auto&y){return x.size()==y.size()&&std::equal(x.begin(),x.end(),y.begin(),[](const auto&l,const auto&r){return l.items==r.items&&l.support==r.support;});};
  const bool apriori=equal(t.itemsets,a),eclat=equal(t.itemsets,e);
  std::print("Original groceries: {} baskets; {} unique incidences; {} initial nonroot nodes; {} frequent sets. Apriori agrees {}; Eclat agrees {}.\n",t.work.transactions,t.work.incidences,t.trees[0].nodes.size()-1,t.itemsets.size(),apriori,eclat);
  std::print("| frequent grocery itemset | support |\n| --- | ---: |\n");for(const auto&f:t.itemsets)std::print("| {} | {} |\n",dm::algorithms::itemset_label(f.items,names),f.support);
  const auto diaper=std::find_if(t.steps.begin(),t.steps.end(),[](const auto&s){return s.tree==0&&s.item==4;});if(diaper==t.steps.end())throw std::runtime_error("missing original suffix");
  std::print("\n| actual diapers ancestor path, nearest first | item names in that order | weight |\n| --- | --- | ---: |\n");for(const auto&path:diaper->base)std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",path.items,dm::algorithms::itemset_label(path.items,names),path.weight);
  const auto&child=t.trees.at(diaper->child);std::print("\nDiapers conditional tree: retained item IDs {}; beer count {}; all actual builds {}; retained history entries {}.\n",child.order,child.nodes.at(1).count,t.work.trees_built,t.captured_entries);
  std::vector<std::size_t>parents;std::vector<std::string>labels;for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.trees[0].nodes.size();++i){const auto&n=t.trees[0].nodes[i];parents.push_back(n.parent);labels.push_back(i?std::format("n{} {}\ncount{}",i,names[n.item],n.count):"n0 root");}
  dm::graphics::Diagram::rooted_tree(parents,labels,"Original actual tree; support descending, ID ties ascending").save_svg("build/fp_growth_original.svg");
  if(t.itemsets.size()!=11||!apriori||!eclat||child.order!=std::vector<std::size_t>{3})throw std::runtime_error("original canonical supports");
}
