> A compact initial tree can have exponentially many answers

Repeat the same U-item basket R times and set the threshold to R. Every nonempty
subset is frequent, so there are F=2^U−1 results despite only U nonroot initial
nodes. Their item payload alone has U*2^(U−1) IDs, because each item belongs to
half of all subsets. No miner returning every itemset can avoid that output size.

For this direct conditional-tree implementation, the dense family also gives
checkable work identities: aggregate nonroot node creations F, builds2^(U−1),
parent hops F−U, peak active frames U and peak frame-resident node records
U*(U+3)/2. The latter sum counts ancestor trees together with the current tree,
including their roots; it excludes temporary child buffers, retained history and
ordinary output. These are exact counts for this family, not general memory bounds.

As a contrast, U disjoint one-item baskets at threshold one have the same U
initial nonroot nodes but only U frequent sets, one build and no parent hops.
The following table reads the executing kernel's counters; the plot's vertical
axis explicitly shows log2 of the untransformed result counts.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fp_growth_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/fp_growth.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/eclat.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double>x,dense_y,disjoint_y;
  std::print("| observed items U | initial dense nodes | dense itemsets | disjoint itemsets | dense created nodes | dense builds | dense parent hops | peak frames | peak frame nodes | output item IDs |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t u=1;u<=10;++u){std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>>dense(5,std::vector<std::size_t>(u)),disjoint(u);for(auto&row:dense)std::iota(row.begin(),row.end(),0);for(std::size_t i=0;i<u;++i)disjoint[i]={i};const auto t=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace(dense,5);const auto d=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_frequent_itemsets_work(disjoint,1);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",u,t.trees[0].nodes.size()-1,t.itemsets.size(),d.itemsets.size(),t.work.nodes_created,t.work.trees_built,t.work.parent_hops,t.work.peak_frames,t.work.peak_live_nodes,t.work.emitted_items);
    x.push_back(double(u));dense_y.push_back(std::log2(double(t.itemsets.size())));disjoint_y.push_back(std::log2(double(d.itemsets.size())));if(t.itemsets.size()!=(std::size_t(1)<<u)-1||d.itemsets.size()!=u)throw std::runtime_error("dense/disjoint pattern counts");}
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(x,dense_y,"Repeated dense baskets",{220,38,38},2).points(x,dense_y,"",{220,38,38},4).lines(x,disjoint_y,"Disjoint singletons",{37,99,235},2).points(x,disjoint_y,"",{37,99,235},4).legend({"Repeated dense baskets","Disjoint singletons"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}}).title("Same initial nonroot node count U; different output sizes").xlab("Observed item count U").ylab("log2 of returned nonempty itemset count").size(1000,520).save_svg("build/fp_growth_work.svg");
}
