\caption(Repeated dense baskets share prefixes well but produce exponentially many sets. Disjoint baskets have the same initial nonroot node count and only linear output. The table also counts all conditional builds and active ancestor-tree nodes.)
\alttext(A native plot compares log-base-two result counts for repeated dense baskets and disjoint singleton baskets with one through ten items; the dense curve reaches log2 of1023 while the disjoint curve reaches log2 of ten.)

The initial tree has at most one nonroot node per retained normalized incidence,
since inserting an item can create at most one node. This does not bound all
simultaneously active ancestor trees, output records or captured history by the
size of one initial tree. Prefix sharing stores the frequent-item projection with
counts; it is not a lossless copy of raw transaction order, duplicate IDs or
discarded items.

Normalization sorts each raw basket. Each build counts weighted item visits,
sorts retained items by support, filters every weighted row, sorts its retained
IDs by rank and inserts them. Hash-map access has expected rather than universal
constant cost. Repeating those operations over conditional databases and copying
all emitted IDs means that an unsorted build is not simply O(N*width), and the
entire mining process is not completed by two scans of the original database.
Two logical passes describe each tree build, after normalization, rather than
every conditional construction combined. The native counters count visits and
records; they exclude sort comparisons, hash internals, allocation and capture copies.

> Online history admission, ownership and threshold zero

FP-growth cannot cheaply know all future conditional trees in advance. Its trace
therefore admits retained history online. The raw base N+raw item occurrences is
checked before copying or normalization. Later records are admitted before their
history copies; a working tree or conditional base may have to be formed first
to determine its size. Failure returns no partial trace.

For the tiny fixture, raw input costs ten entries, tree snapshots nineteen and
step records with their paths nine: total38. This cap covers retained history,
not ordinary canonical output, active working trees, normalized input, conditional
buffers or allocator bytes. The units are records and array entries, not bytes or
peak RAM. The default is one million entries; zero disables only the optional cap,
while checked arithmetic and container bounds remain.

Threshold zero preserves the historical FP API: only positive-support co-occurring
sets are returned. Apriori and Eclat instead include all nonempty observed-universe
subsets meeting zero, including unsupported combinations. The disjoint two-item
control below therefore gives two FP sets and three from each other miner.
Their complete-output equivalence claim requires a positive threshold.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fp_growth_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/fp_growth.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/eclat.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> rows{{0,1},{0,1},{0},{1}};const auto original=rows;const auto bound=std::size_t(38);bool rejected=false;try{dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace(rows,2,bound-1);}catch(const std::length_error&){rejected=true;}const auto t=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace(rows,2,bound);rows[0].clear();const bool owned=t.transactions==original&&t.steps[1].base[0].weight==2;
  std::print("Online tiny history: raw base {}; captured {}; one below rejected {}; trees {}; steps {}; owned after caller edits {}.\n",dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace_base_entries(original),t.captured_entries,rejected,t.trees.size(),t.steps.size(),owned);
  const auto big=std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max();const auto wide=dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace({{big,big},{},{0,big},{0}},0);const bool real_item=std::any_of(wide.trees[0].nodes.begin()+1,wide.trees[0].nodes.end(),[&](const auto&n){return n.item==big;});
  std::print("Full-width item control: real SIZE_MAX node {}; frequent sets {}; empty trace entries {}; above-row-count result size {}.\n",real_item,wide.itemsets.size(),dm::algorithms::fp_growth_trace({},0,2).captured_entries,dm::algorithms::fp_growth_frequent_itemsets(original,big).size());
  const std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> disjoint{{0},{1}};const std::vector<std::vector<dm::algorithms::FrequentItemset>> results{dm::algorithms::fp_growth_frequent_itemsets(disjoint,0),dm::algorithms::apriori_frequent_itemsets(disjoint,0),dm::algorithms::eclat_frequent_itemsets(disjoint,0)};const std::vector<std::string> names{"FP-growth","Apriori","Eclat"};std::vector<double>counts;
  std::print("| miner at threshold0 | returned nonempty sets | returned zero-support sets |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");for(std::size_t i=0;i<results.size();++i){const auto zero=std::count_if(results[i].begin(),results[i].end(),[](const auto&f){return f.support==0;});std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",names[i],results[i].size(),zero);counts.push_back(double(results[i].size()));}
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(counts,names);plot.title("Historical threshold0 behavior on two disjoint items").xlab("Native miner").ylab("Returned nonempty itemsets").y_limits(0,3.5).size(1000,500).save_svg("build/fp_growth_controls.svg");
  if(!rejected||!owned||!real_item||results[0].size()!=2||results[1].size()!=3||results[2].size()!=3)throw std::runtime_error("owned history and zero support controls");
}
```
